                ext4).
    @alloc      the cut stem and the name, returned.
    """
    suffix = MESSAGE_SUFFIX if name.endswith(MESSAGE_SUFFIX) else ""  # SHARED fix round 5 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): LOW-2
    stem = name[:len(name) - len(suffix)]
    room = limit - len(os.fsencode(BY_HAND_NAME.format(k=k, name=suffix)))
    kept = 0
    for c in stem:
        size = len(os.fsencode(c))
        if size > room:
            break
        room -= size
        kept += 1
    return BY_HAND_NAME.format(k=k, name=stem[:kept] + suffix)


# SHARED fix round 3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's MEDIUM-1, tc read never replaces a file in read/.
def by_hand_target(path):
    """Where tc read moves the inbox file at `path` when read/ already holds its name: read/BY_HAND_NAME, k the first count from 1 that nothing holds.

    For cmd_read, under the inbox lock it holds alone. Each name is
    checked with lstat, never followed. SHARED fix round 4 (Claude Opus
    5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1. Each name is cut to fit read/'s
    name limit (os.pathconf PC_NAME_MAX, by_hand_name), and only
    FileNotFoundError makes a name free. Any other OSError (the name limit
    or a by-hand name tc cannot look at) is raised, never taken as free,
    so the search always ends and cmd_read names the file, left unread.

    @interacts  reads read/'s name limit (pathconf); lstats
                read/by-hand-<k>-<name> for k = 1, 2, ... until one is
                free. Writes nothing. Raises OSError as above.
    @deps       by_hand_name; os, pathlib.
    @complexity O(t * c) over t names by-hand-<k>-<name> already in read/
                (none in an honest mailbox; each one a file someone made by
                hand) and c characters of the name (by_hand_name).
    @alloc      one path per k tried; the free one, returned.
    """
    read = path.parent / "read"
    limit = os.pathconf(read, "PC_NAME_MAX")
    k = 1
    while True:
        target = read / by_hand_name(k, path.name, limit)
        try:
            os.lstat(target)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return target
        k += 1


# SHARED fix round 4 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's MEDIUM-1, owners as plain facts.
def owner_words(path):
    """` (owned by <login>)` for the entry at `path`, lstat()ed, never followed; an empty string when lstat cannot reach it.

    @interacts  lstats `path`; reads the user database.
    @deps       user_name, printable; os.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      one short string, returned.
    """
    try:
        return f" (owned by {printable(user_name(os.lstat(path).st_uid))})"
    except OSError:
        return ""


# SHARED fix round 5 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-1, a rename that did nothing.
def still_in_inbox(path, target):
    """Words when the inbox file at `path` is still in the inbox after tc read renamed it to `target`; None when it left.

    rename(2) between two names of one file does nothing and returns 0.
    So a member who hard-links their own inbox file at `target`, between
    tc read's lstat of that name and its rename, keeps the file in the
    inbox while the rename reports success. lstat finds `path` gone: it
    moved. Still there as the same file as `target` (os.path.samestat),
    or with `target` gone: not moved. Still there as another file: the
    move was made and a new file took the name after it; the next read
    meets that one.

    @interacts  lstats `path` and, when it is still there, `target`.
                Writes nothing. Raises OSError when lstat cannot reach
                either for any reason but absence.
    @deps       printable; os.
    @complexity O(1): at most two lstat calls.
    @alloc      one string or none, returned.
    """
    try:
        here = os.lstat(path)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    try:
        if not os.path.samestat(here, os.lstat(target)):
            return None
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    return (f"it is still in the inbox after the move to {printable(target)}: a hard link to it, made there "
            f"just before, makes a move do nothing")


def cmd_read(args):
    """`tc read <name>`: print unread mail and move it to read/.

    The lock covers the glob and the renames only. The mail is printed
    from read/ after the lock is released, so a reader whose stdout stalls
    (a pager left open, a Ctrl-Z) never blocks a send to this name. The
    price: a print that fails after the renames leaves that mail in read/
    unseen, where `tc log` still shows it.

    Only regular files move (D19). A symlink, a FIFO or a folder named
    like a message is left in place, named on stderr and never read; so is
    a moved file read_regular_file will not read. Either makes the read
    exit 1 after it prints the rest. SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5,
    unreviewed): so does a message the rename may not move. Under a shared
    mailbox (D20) only the inbox's owner moves another member's mail, so a
    read run by anyone else moves only that user's own messages and names
    the rest, left unread, never a traceback.

    SHARED fix round 3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's MEDIUM-1
    (D20). A rename replaces whatever holds its target, so before each move
    tc lstats read/<name>. When that name is taken (a member made a file
    under a message's name in the inbox or in read/, which the group may
    write under C4), the inbox file moves to the first free
    read/by-hand-<k>-<name> instead (BY_HAND_NAME, by_hand_target).
    Nothing in read/ is replaced. The note is words, not a refusal: the
    file is shown and moved, and the read exits 0 for it. The lstat and
    the rename are two steps, and that is safe here: only tc read moves
    mail into read/, and only under this lock, so a name found free cannot
    turn into a message before the rename. A member may make new names in
    read/ (group write), but under sticky only a file's owner (its sender)
    or read/'s owner (the reader) may replace or remove a file there; so a
    name found free and taken before the rename can only be that member's
    own new file, and the rename replaces nothing but the planter's own.

    SHARED fix round 4 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1
    and MEDIUM-1 (D20). The by-hand name is cut to fit read/'s name limit
    (by_hand_name), so a planted name of any length moves, the inbox
    empties, and tc wait wakes for it at most once. tc cannot tell which
    of the two files it wrote, so the note, printed just before the moved
    file, names neither as the forgery: two files had the name, tc never
    reuses a number, so one of them was not written by tc, and both are
    kept, at read/<name> (unchanged) and at the by-hand name (the one from
    the inbox, shown below). Under a shared mailbox it adds each file's
    owner as a plain fact (owner_words), with no verdict. A by-hand search
    that fails (by_hand_target raises) files the message as not moved.

    SHARED fix round 5 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-1.
    "At most once" holds unless a member wins a race: a hard link made at
    the chosen name between its lstat and the rename turns rename(2) into
    nothing, and the file stays in the inbox. So after each rename tc
    read lstats the old name (still_in_inbox), and a file still there as
    the same file is filed as not moved, with words, never shown or
    claimed as read. Each race a member wins costs one more wake, the
    floor D20 allows, never a loop.

    @interacts  checks the name first (C0, exit 6, nothing written); a name
                with no inbox gets `no unread mail` and nothing is created
                (O2); else reads ROOT/.shared (mailbox_share), holds
                mail/.box-<name>.lock alone from its glob to its last rename
                (D14; held past LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS by another process, exit
                10, nothing moved: inbox_lock, D20, D23), refuses an inbox or
                read/ that is a symlink, or under
                a shared mailbox not its card owner's (exit 1, nothing
                moved: check_inbox, D17, D20), or that it cannot list
                (exit 1, inbox_messages, SHARED fix round 3), in a private mailbox makes
                mail/to-<name>/read/ if missing, lstats each *.md entry,
                lstats read/<name> for each regular file (and, when it is
                taken, reads read/'s name limit and lstats
                read/by-hand-<k>-<name> until one is free) and
                renames the file there, then lstats the old name (and,
                when something is still there, the target:
                still_in_inbox); then, with the lock released,
                prints the by-hand note before each file that has one
                (under a shared mailbox lstatting both files and reading
                the user database for their owners), reads each moved
                file (read_regular_file) and prints to stdout, and names
                each one it will not show or could not move on stderr.
    @deps       require_names, mailbox_share, inbox_lock, inbox_messages,
                by_hand_target, still_in_inbox, owner_words, show,
                refuse_lines, printable; os.
    @complexity O(u + t * c) over u *.md entries in the inbox (single
                digits), t names by-hand-<k>-<name> already in read/ (none
                in an honest mailbox) and c characters of a by-hand name
                (at most 255).
    @alloc      the lists of paths and each file's text while printed; one
                open lock file while the lock is held.
    """
    require_names(args.name)  # CARDS R1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed)
    if not (MAIL / f"to-{args.name}").is_dir():  # CARDS R3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): O2
        print(f"no unread mail for {args.name}")
        return
    # CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D14. A codex send
    # cannot land between this glob and these renames, so it never skips its
    # ring for mail this read did not print.
    # Phase fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): print after the
    # release, so a stalled stdout holds no lock.
    shared = mailbox_share() is not None  # SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20
    with inbox_lock(args.name):
        messages, skipped = inbox_messages(args.name, shared)  # SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D19
        moved, stuck = [], []
        for p in messages:
            target, twin = p.parent / "read" / p.name, None
            try:  # SHARED fix round 4 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): HIGH-1, a failed by-hand search is stuck, never a traceback
                if os.path.lexists(target):  # SHARED fix round 3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): MEDIUM-1, never over read/
                    twin, target = target, by_hand_target(p)
                p.rename(target)
                left = still_in_inbox(p, target)  # SHARED fix round 5 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): LOW-1, a rename that did nothing
            except OSError as err:  # SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20, not this user's to move
                stuck.append((p, err.strerror))
                continue
            if left is not None:
                stuck.append((p, left))
                continue
            moved.append((target, twin))
    if not moved and not stuck:  # mail it could not move is still unread
        print(f"no unread mail for {args.name}")
    unshown = []
    for path, twin in moved:
        if twin is not None:  # SHARED fix round 4 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): MEDIUM-1, neither file named the forgery
            print(f"tc read: two files had the name {printable(twin.name)}, one in read/ and one in the inbox. "
                  f"tc never reuses a number, so one of them was not written by tc. Both are kept: "
                  f"{printable(twin)}{owner_words(twin) if shared else ''}, unchanged, and "
                  f"{printable(path)}{owner_words(path) if shared else ''}, from the inbox, shown below:")
        unshown += show([path])
    refuse_lines([f"tc read: {printable(p)} not shown and left in place ({why})" for p, why in skipped]
                 + [f"tc read: {printable(p)} not moved to read/ ({why}); left unread" for p, why in stuck]
                 + [f"tc read: {printable(p)} not shown ({why})" for p, why in unshown])


def cmd_peek(args):
    """`tc peek <name>`: print unread mail and leave it unread.

    Only regular files count and print (D19). A symlink, a FIFO or a
    folder named like a message is named on stderr and never read, and the
    peek exits 1 after it prints the rest.

    @interacts  checks the name first (C0, exit 6, nothing written); a name
                with no inbox gets `no unread mail` and nothing is created
                (O2); else reads ROOT/.shared (inbox_messages), refuses an
                inbox or read/ that is a symlink, or under a shared mailbox
                not its card owner's (exit 1, nothing read: check_inbox,
                D17, D20), or that it cannot list (exit 1, inbox_messages,
                SHARED fix round 3), in a private mailbox makes mail/to-<name>/read/
                if missing, lstats each *.md entry and reads each regular
                file (read_regular_file); prints to stdout, and names each
